# Welcome to Markdown Viewer 3.10.1

_Released August 24, 2026_

This release makes review feel native to the document, puts everyday controls where you expect them, and gives keyboard-focused writers a much faster path through Markdown.

Everything here ships with the web, PWA, Docker, and desktop editions. **Happy coding!**

[View release on GitHub](https://github.com/ThisIs-Developer/Markdown-Viewer/releases/tag/v3.10.1) [Open the full changelog](https://github.com/ThisIs-Developer/Markdown-Viewer/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md)

---

## Highlights

- **Comment on the exact content that matters.** Select words or a rendered element, preview the anchor, and start a focused conversation without changing the Markdown.
- **A calmer, more capable interface.** Document commands are consolidated, formatting tools are easier to scan, and active, disabled, dropdown, theme, and Private mode states are clearer.
- **Keyboard-first editing.** Familiar formatting, find, navigation, import, export, view, Explorer, and fullscreen shortcuts now cover the most common writing workflows.
- **More dependable workspaces.** Multi-file dragging behaves as one operation, expanded folders are easier drop targets, and document statistics stay accurate when content renders or reopens.
- **A broader and better-tested release.** Bulgarian joins the interface languages, current release notes are available inside the app, and a locked local Playwright suite covers core browser behavior.

> [!TIP]
> Open **Comments**, select text in Preview, and choose **New** to try the redesigned review flow. Press `Ctrl+Enter` or `Cmd+Enter` to submit.

## Comments that stay with the content

Comments now begin with a real selection instead of a generic feedback form. Select text in a heading, paragraph, list, quote, or code block, or choose a rendered image, diagram, map, model, or math expression. A temporary highlight confirms the anchor before you submit.

The document and Comments panel stay synchronized: hover either side to find its match, or activate a card to bring the conversation into focus. Open, Resolved, and All filters keep completed work available without crowding current feedback.

### Conversations and collaboration

- Add nested replies from the active comment card.
- Edit or delete replies and edit, resolve, reopen, or delete the original comment.
- Keep selected text and draft focus stable while the panel changes state.
- Use a desktop side panel, tablet drawer, or mobile bottom sheet.
- Show each Live Share participant's current display name on comments and replies, including view-only participants.
- Continue reading older stored comments and suggestions after the upgrade.

Comments remain local to normal documents unless Live Share is active. They stay out of Markdown and document exports, duplicated tabs, and Share Snapshot links.

## A clearer workspace

The header now groups document-level actions into a consistent toolbar for view modes, New, Copy, scroll sync, Comments, Share Snapshot, Live Share, and Export. The application logo remains visible, dropdowns move more smoothly, and buttons communicate selected, expanded, or unavailable states more clearly.

The formatting toolbar has matching groups for history, text style, paragraph formatting, content insertion, technical content, additional insertion, Find and Replace, and fullscreen. Heading, text-case, alignment, and direction choices use compact menus so the most frequent tools stay within reach.

Theme and Private mode switches now use clearer icon and color states. Read-only shared documents and the Release Notes tab keep unavailable document commands visible but disabled, which preserves the layout while explaining what cannot be changed.

## Faster writing and reliable feedback

Markdown Viewer now supports a wider familiar shortcut set across Windows, Linux, and macOS. Browser-reserved shortcuts remain desktop-only where intercepting them on the web would disrupt normal browser tabs.

### New shortcut coverage

- Toggle bold, italic, strikethrough, inline code, links, code blocks, blockquotes, and ordered or bulleted lists.
- Apply paragraph or heading levels on desktop and clear formatting from the current selection or line.
- Open quick file search, import Markdown, export Markdown, Settings, Find and Replace, or fullscreen.
- Toggle Edit/Preview, Explorer, and synchronized scrolling.
- Create, close, and move between documents with desktop-friendly bindings and web-safe alternatives.

Word count, character count, and reading time now refresh on every render, including after switching to or reopening a document. Markdown copy feedback also waits for the clipboard operation to succeed before reporting completion.

## Explorer, language, and release updates

Dragging any file in a multi-file selection now moves the whole selection and shows the correct file count. Expanded folders accept drops across their visible contents, reducing the precision needed to organize a busy workspace.

Bulgarian is now available from the language menu, bringing Markdown Viewer to 15 interface languages. The Bulgarian catalog follows the same generated-key parity and audit checks as the existing translations.

Release notes now open inside Markdown Viewer as a temporary, read-only workspace tab. New users see the note beside Welcome without losing editor focus; existing users see it after an upgrade. Closing the tab marks that version as seen, and **About Markdown Viewer > Show Release Notes** brings it back whenever you want it.

## Quality and compatibility

Contributors now have a locked Playwright setup with a cross-platform local server, static asset and syntax checks, Chromium end-to-end coverage, and cross-browser smoke tests. The suite covers workspace, editor, review, export, sharing, rich content, responsive storage, GitHub import, and toolbar behavior without depending on live third-party services.

This release also updates DOMPurify and js-yaml to patched versions, corrects integrity-checked browser asset sources, keeps desktop offline dependencies synchronized, restores WebKit support for local HTTP development, and adds an explicit application-ready lifecycle for dependable automation.

